# Where the order of a procedure's blocks had to be made again, its allocation, references and frame are checked in every build. `total` calls on every way, so it sets up its frame at entry and has no framed part: nothing else asks for a check of it. Its loop keeps a pointer into the storage of `list` across its poll, so `list` is kept for the collector in a slot that no step reads again. THE LIE, testing=ValuesKeptForTheCollectorAreTakenToBeReadByStepsAlone: the allocator takes that slot to be read by nobody and leaves out its store. testing=OrderBlocksAgain orders the blocks as they are ordered the second time.
# No check is asked for here, as in a build of a user: the build stops by name because the order was made again. The same lie without that word: an-owner-whose-slot-is-left-unwritten-is-not-seen-where-nothing-checks-the-allocation.ir.
# f1 = total
# -- source: an-owner-whose-slot-is-left-unwritten-stops-the-build-by-name-where-the-order-of-blocks-was-made-again.ts
# declare function print(value: unknown): void;
# function total(list: number[]): number { print(0); let sum = 0; for (let i = 0; i < list.length; i++) sum = (sum + list[i]) | 0; print(sum); return sum; }
# print(total([1, 2, 3]));
# -- end of source
# REQUIRES: x86_64
# AS-USERS-BUILD: the checks of a procedure whose order was made again run in every build, and under the check of the runner they run everywhere
# DIAGNOSTIC: InnerPointerWithoutOwner f1
# RUN: emit f1 mechanisms=all testing=OrderBlocksAgain,ValuesKeptForTheCollectorAreTakenToBeReadByStepsAlone
